\subsection{EFO Evaluation}
\label{sec:emotion_detection}

A graph-based emotion detector for EFO has been implemented on top of the FRED knowledge extractor \cite{gangemi2017semantic}, \cite{DBLP:journals/semweb/GangemiPRNDM17}. FRED can be considered as a ``situation analyzer'': it performs hybrid knowledge extraction from natural language, based on both statistical and rule-based components, which generate RDF/OWL knowledge graphs, including entity linking, word-sense disambiguation, and frame/semantic role detection. Being directly linked to the Framester ontology, its graphs include: (i) word sense disambiguation to the WordNet resource, (ii) VerbNet verb sense disambiguation, including their semantic roles;
%attribution on the semantic arguments structure of the sentence; 
(iii) frame detection from FrameNet; (iv) PropBank frame recognition; (v) DBpedia entity linking.
FRED is available as an online service and as an API\footnote{\url{http://wit.istc.cnr.it/stlab-tools/fred/demo/}}.


The graph-based detection process is composed of the following three main steps: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A sentence in natural language is given as input to FRED tool. FRED parses the sentence and builds a knowledge graph of semantic dependencies, performing frame extraction, WordNet disambiguation, entity recognition, etc.
    \item 
    %The second step consists in navigating the graph produced by FRED and, out of all the subjects, predicates, and objects consider only entities from FrameNet, WordNet, VerbNet, DBpedia and other possible emotion triggers, as mentioned in Sect. \ref{sec:bet}. 
    For each Framester node in the graph, we perform a SPARQL query to the BET graph, to extract emotion triggers
    \item For each emotion trigger, a triple is added to the original graph declaring which emotion is triggered.
\end{enumerate}

We tested the graph-based detector on the WASSA 2017 dataset \cite{mohammad2017wassa}, a corpus generated in the context of the 8th Workshop on Computational Approaches to Subjectivity, Sentiment and Social Media Analysis. 
This dataset was chosen because, unlike many others, is annotated on each single sentence, and not e.g. for an entire dialogue. %Furthermore, the two versions (2017, 2023) are chosen since the first one is composed of very short tweets, while the second one includes longer sms messages.
%In fact, the problem presented in the previous chapter, concerning the significance of a certain annotation, also occurs here: an exchange of jokes between two individuals can be humorous without there being any lexical or explicative factual triggers in the sentence. At this stage of development of the detector we are not able to make such complex inferences, and the aim of these experiments was primarily to test automatic detection and then use it to support the exploration of value knowledge, which is why only Ekman’s Basic Emotions theory was operationalised. 

%Although a good proportion of these, especially the first few hundred, are clearly related in various ways to the emotion in question, the quality of the dataset cannot be said to be the best, since it is generated from collecting tweets with chosen hashtags. 
%For this reason, in the file preprocessing, we decided to strip the hashtag character ’\#’, but leave the hashtag string, since many of the tweets were showing just a few words, and their whole meaning was depending on the hashtag as semantic qualifier and disclaimer both for the topic and the possible senses of the sentence. We provide detection specifications in the following paragraphs.
The WASSA2017 corpus provides 4 datasets, annotated with 4 of Ekman’s basic emotions: fear, anger, enjoyment and sadness. Each dataset consists of approximately 1000 tweets, all labeled with one of the emotions.
%In the data preprocessing phase, we decided to strip the hashtag character '\#', but leave the hashtag sting, since many of the tweets consists of a few words, and their whole meaning depend on the hashtag.

