\section{Introduction}
% Grammatical Error Correction (GEC) has long been a critical task in natural language processing (NLP) and computer-assisted language learning (CALL), with significant progress made in correcting written text such as essays and emails~\cite{bryant2022grammatical, bryant2023}. Several shared tasks have been released in the past years~\cite{ng2014conll,bryant2019bea,multigec2024}, which have advanced the research on written GEC. However, as interest grows in automating all skills in language learning, including speaking, attention has shifted towards spoken grammatical error correction (SGEC) — a domain that presents distinct and complex challenges compared to its written counterpart.

