\section{Spoken GEC System}
\label{sec:gec_systems}
Recent advances in large-scale speech foundation models, particularly Whisper \cite{radford2023robust}, have opened new possibilities for end-to-end spoken language processing. Whisper is trained on over 680,000 hours of multilingual audio with a multi-task learning objective, enabling it to generalize effectively across a wide range of tasks, including low-resource ASR \cite{qian2024learn, timmel2024fine}, speech translation \cite{ma2025cross}, and spoken language understanding \cite{ma2024investigating, goron2024improving}.
%
In this work, Whisper serves as the backbone for all three SGEC architectures explored: cascaded, partial-cascaded, and end-to-end.
%
% While prior research has provided early insights into these configurations~\cite{banno2024towards, qian2025scaling}, our study goes further by conducting a comprehensive comparison and enhancement of each architecture. In particular, we introduce novel contributions that improve SGEC and feedback generation performance, including (1) a large-scale pseudo-labeling strategy to leverage unlabeled audio data, (2) prompt-based training with fluent transcriptions to provide contextual cues, (3) a reference alignment method to refine feedback quality, and (4) an edit confidence estimation and filtering framework to exclude low-confidence edit for evaluation.
