The runtime of a \tdfa[k] $M$ with a supersafe head must have a linear upper bound, since the \tdfa{} $N_i$ corresponding to the supersafe head is guaranteed to halt for all inputs, any \tdfa{} running on an input of length $n$ can attain $\OH{n}$ different configurations, and \Cref{def:supersafe} guarantees that $M$'s runtime is not longer than that of $N_i$.%the  property halts in finite $\abs{T_{w,i}}$ steps, $N_i$ must be running in linear time.  Since $H_i$ traces only a prefix of $T_{w,i}$ in any simulation of $M$ as described in the second property, $M$ also must be running in at most $\abs{T_{w,i}}$ steps, therefore in linear time.

Simulations as described in the second property are analogous to those in the definition of safe heads found in~\cite{GS22}.\footnote{Readers consulting to~\cite{GS22} should beware that $M_i$ found in the definition of safe heads in~\cite{GS22} are different than $N_i$ described in the definition of supersafe heads here.!!!BU DİPNOT VE GELDİĞİ PARAGRAF SONRA BİR ELDEN GEÇER!!!}  Since those simulations are always halting, a supersafe head is always a safe head.

Whether it is possible to devise a general algorithm that decides if a given \tdfa[k] has a supersafe head is, for now, an open question.

