By filtering out the inputs that are not padded strings at \Cref{stg:ssfilter}, $V$ essentially challenges its prover to make a claim about the core's membership in $L$ and checks if it is true by simulating $M$ on the core for several rounds.  Curiously enough, $V$ makes this simulation in collaboration with the prover, checking on the prover's claim with the prover's help.  Naturally, $V$ is very careful about trusting this help, particularly because $V$ may be made to run indefinitely whenever it blindly trusts what the prover says.  To that end, $V$ puts in its trust to this help with a very low probability $p \in \paren*{0, 0.5}$, validating that it is indeed what it is supposed to be (\ie, $H_1$'s readings) with the remaining high probability, $1-p$.

The following analysis will first demonstrate a \tqfa{} $P$ that passes $V$'s tests for an infinite set of input strings with high probability.  Then, we will show that any \tpfa{} $P^*$ will fail same tests for another infinite set of input strings, again with high probability.  Showing that those sets always have an infinite intersection will conclude the proof.

\paragraph{A \tqfa{} prover $P$.}  We propose the following algorithm for the \tqfa{} prover $P$:
\begin{turing}{P}{On input $w$:}
    \item Execute $T$'s algorithm on the core of $w$ as long as $V$ is asking for $P$'s decision, answering it with the \msg{not yet} option until $T$ halts or $V$ sends a forced\-/claim.
    If $T$ halts first, then pass on $T$'s decision to $V$ with the corresponding message, immediately.
    If $V$ sends a forced\-/claim first (which indicates that the time is up), then stop executing $T$.
    \item Do the following repeatedly, regardless of what $T$'s claim or $V$'s forced\-/claim is:
    \bitem{Move the input head to \lend.}
    \bitem{Wait for $V$ to send the message \msg{\lend{} is reached}.}
    \bitem{Simulate $N_1$ and send its readings to $V$ until $V$ responds \msg{this round is over}.}
\end{turing}

Let $\verracc_V$ denote the error upper bound of $V$ failing to accept with $P$.  Following through the algorithms of $V$ and $P$ step\-/by\-/step, one can see that $V$ accepts with $P$ if (but not only if) all of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The input string $w$ is in $\padlang{\Sigma^*}$.
    \item The time allotted to $P$ suffices for $T$'s simulation over $x$ (the core of $w$) to be finished.
    \item $T$ makes the correct decision about $x$'s membership in $L$.
\end{enumerate}
Let
\begin{equation*}
    S^+_T = \Set{ xy | 
    \forall x \in \Sigma^*,
    \forall y \in \Set{\padsymb}^*,
    \abs{xy} \ge 2^{k_T\abs{x} + c_T}
    }.
\end{equation*}
The set $S^+_T$ is a subset of $\padlang{\Sigma^*}$, so the first of the three conditions hold with an input string chosen from $S^+_T$.  Moreover, given an input string from $S^+_T$, $V$ will grant $P$ $2^{k_T\abs{x} + c_T + 1}$ timesteps to decide.  $T$ will complete its execution on $x$ with at least $1 - \tau_{k_T,c_T}$ probability when given this much time, by its definition.  Lastly, $T$'s decision will be correct with a probability at least $1 - \verr_T$.  Therefore, the overall probability of $V$ accepting with $P$ when given an input string from the set $S^+_T$ is at least
\begin{equation*}
    \paren*{1 - \tau_{k_T,c_T}} \cdot \paren*{1 - \verr_T} = 
    1 - \tau_{k_T,c_T} - \verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T} \verr_T.
\end{equation*}
Then, the probability of failing at that is at most
\begin{equation*}
    \tau_{k_T,c_T} + \verr_T - \tau_{k_T,c_T} \verr_T \le \tau_{k_T,c_T} + \verr_T.
\end{equation*}
Thus, the upper bound for the probability of failing to accept, $\verracc_V$, can be lowered arbitrarily by lowering $\tau_{k_T,c_T}$ and $\verr_T$.


