\paragraph{Any \tpfa{} prover $P^*$.}  Let $\verrrej_V$ denote the error bound of $V$ failing to reject any \tpfa{} prover.

First, note that $L \in \asd$ and SOMELEMMA together imply that no \tpfa{} can separate $\padlang{L}$ from $\padlang{\setneg{L}}$, meaning that any \tpfa{} is bound to make error with at least $\sfrac12$ probability in identifying whether a given input string $w \in \padlang{\Sigma^*}$ is in $\padlang{L}$ or $\padlang{\setneg{L}}$, for infinitely many strings $w$.  For any \tpfa{} $P^*$, let $S^-_{P^*}$ be the infinite set of all such strings.

Second, provers of $V$ are expected to provide readings of $N_1$ in both \Cref{stg:SIMN1,stg:SIMM}.  At those stages, the symbols that $P^*$ provide will either match or do not match the readings of $N_1$.  In the following analysis, let $t_i$ denote the probability of the event that the symbols provided by $P^*$ matches the readings of $N_1$ when $V$ is on its \ith{i} round of verification.

Now, given a string in $S^-_{P^*}$ as input to $V$, $P^*$ will be mistaken about its membership with a probability at least $\sfrac12$.  When $P^*$ is indeed mistaken about the membership, one of the following four things may happen in each round $i$ of $V$'s verification:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $V$ may simulate $N_1$ while $P^*$ is telling symbols that match.  In this case, $V$ will advance to the next round.  The probability of this event is $\paren*{1-p}t_i$.
    \item $V$ may simulate $N_1$ while $P^*$ is telling symbols that are not in complete match.  In this case, $V$ will reject.  The probability of this event is $\paren*{1-p}\paren*{1-t_i}$.
    \item $V$ may simulate $M$ while $P^*$ is telling symbols that match.  In this case, $V$ will reject.  The probability of this event is $pt_i$.
    \item $V$ may simulate $M$ while $P^*$ is telling symbols that are not in complete match.  In this case, $V$ may possibly get stuck simulating $M$ indefinitely.  The probability of this event is $p\paren*{1-t_i}$.
\end{enumerate}
