\end{equation*}
(We use ellipsis to hide the crowded list of parameters.) Observe that $\delta(1, \dotsc) = p^2$ is positive.  We see that $\delta(0, \dotsc) = (1-p)(1 - p - f(i+1))$ is also positive when we assume $f(i+1) < 1-p$, the antecedent of the claim.  Since $\delta$ is a linear function in terms of $t_i$ and is positive at the boundaries $t_i = 0$ and $t_i = 1$, it is positive everywhere in between as well.  Since the difference is positive without any further constraints over $p$ and $f(i+1)$, we reach the strict inequality that is claimed in the first half of the consequent.

Let us now examine the right\-/hand sides of the two inequalities in the consequent. Specifically, how large $(1-p)(p + f(i+1))$ is from $f(i+1)$:
\begin{equation*}
    (1-p)(p + f(i+1)) - f(i+1) = pd_{i+1}
\end{equation*}
Combining this with the first consequent we had reached, we conclude with the second.
\end{subproof}

\Cref{clm:iterationalimprovement} essentially states that the distance $d_i$ between $1-p$ and $f(i)$'s closes up with each recursive step, at a rate that is greater than being multiplied by $1-p$.  For $f(m)$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    d_m = \paren*{1-p} - (1-p)(1-t_m) - pt_m =
    t_m(1-2p),
\end{equation*}
and with the rate that the distance closes up, we have
\begin{align*}
    d_1 &< t_m(1-2p) \cdot \paren*{1-p}^{m-1}\\
    \paren*{1-p} - f(1) &< t_m(1-2p) \cdot \paren*{1-p}^{m-1}\\
    f(1) &> \paren*{1-p} - t_m(1-2p) \cdot \paren*{1-p}^{m-1}.
\end{align*}
Knowing that $t_m$ can at most be $1$, one can ensure a lower bound for $f(1)$ that is arbitrarily high by decreasing $p$ to bring the positive term arbitrarily close to $1$ and increasing $m$ to bring the negative term arbitrarily close to $0$.  This increases the rejection probability of the $\paren*{V, P^*}$ pair arbitrarily close to $\sfrac12$; thus, the upper bound for the probability of failing to reject, $\verrrej_V$, can be lowered arbitrarily close to $\sfrac12$.
