    For any string $x \in (\Sigma \cup \{ENDMARKER\})^*$, the \emph{string probabilities of $M$ on $x$} are defined as follows. A \emph{beginning condition} is a pair $(g,\xi)$ where $g$ is a megastate of $M$ and $\xi \in \{\text{Left, Right}\}$, meaning ``$M$ starts at megastate $g$ with its head on the $\xi$ end of $x$''. An \emph{ending condition} is either
    \begin{itemize}
        \item a pair $(g,\xi)$ as above, meaning ``the head of $M$ walks off the $\xi$ end of $x$, while it is at megastate $g$,''
        \item ``Loop'', meaning ``$M$ runs forever without its head leaving $x$,''
        \item ``Accept'', meaning ``$M$ accepts before its head leaves $x$,'' or
        \item ``Reject'', meaning ``$M$ rejects before its head leaves $x$,''.
    \end{itemize}

    For each beginning condition $B$ and ending condition $E$, let $p(x,B,E)$ be the probability that $E$ occurs given that $M$ is started on $B$. For each $x$, the number $d(n)$ of these string probabilities is $\OH{c(n)^2}$, so $d(n)<(\log n)^a$ for all positive $a$ and almost all $n$ as well. Let $\mathbf{p}(x)$ be the vector of these $d(n)$ probabilities according to some ordering.
    For any string $w  \in W_m$, consider the Markov chain $K(w)$ whose states are of the form $(g,l)$, where $g$ is a megastate of $M$ and $0\leq l \leq m+1$. The chain state $(g,l)$ with $1\leq l \leq m\}$ corresponds to $M$ being in megastate $g$ with the head on the \ith{l} symbol of $w$.  Transition probabilities from such states can be read from the transition function of $M$ easily. Chain states of the form $(g,l)$ with $l \in \Set{0,m+1}$ are trap states of $K(w)$ and correspond to $M$'s head walking off $w$. As described in~\cite{DS92}, the nonzero elements of $\mathbf{p}(w)$ correspond to paths of length at most $c(n)m$ in $K(w)$, and must therefore have value at least $\tau^{c(n)m}$.% \geq \tau^{c(n)n}$.

    For an arbitrary $m\in I$, we divide $W_m$ into equivalence classes by making $w$ and $w'$ equivalent if $\mathbf{p}(w)$ and \textbf{p}($w'$) are zero in exactly the same places. Let $E_m$ be a largest equivalence class, so $\abs{E_m}\geq \frac{\abs{W_m}}{2^{d(n)}}$.

