    It is imperative to note that this likelihood can never reach $1$, since the nominator is always strictly smaller than the denominator.
\end{enumerate}

It follows from this last note that whenever $P^*$ decides to tell its lie, it will be taking some non-zero risk of being caught and rejected while doing it, however small.  Moreover, since $V$ is always tracing one of the safe heads and providing them correctly by the input, the simulation of $M$ on any segment will always halt in \OH{n} steps; thus $P^*$'s lie cannot make $V$ loop.  Let $w_1$ be a segmented input string with which the probability of $P^*$ being rejected is $p > 0$.  Then, with the input string $\paren*{w_1\segsep}^k$, the rejection probability of $P^*$ will become $1 - \paren*{1-p}^k$, which will get arbitrarily closer to $1$ with increasing $k$.




