which makes $V$ pass every segment\footnote{Since the readings for the safe head are provided correctly to the simulation in either branch of the verification, the simulations will halt in linear time in both.  Since the simulation will be carried out faithfully in the second branch, the simulated $M$'s decision will be correct and matching against $P$'s claim based on $T$'s recognition of $L$.} and leads it to conviction about $P$'s quantumness with certainty.

\newcommand{\trouble}{\ensuremath{S_{P^*}}}

Let $P^*$ be any \tpfa{} prover.  Since $L$ cannot be recognized by any \tpfa{} by definition, for any $P^*$, once constructed, there has to be an infinite set of strings $\trouble$ for which $P^*$'s claim for membership of any $s \in \trouble$ is incorrect with probability at least $0.5$.\footnote{To attain the minimum of $0.5$ probability of being incorrect, $P^*$ can toss a fair coin to determine its claim for the membership of all $s \in \trouble$.}  Given a string $s \in \trouble$, if a $P^*$ reports the readings of $M$'s safe head truthfully and $V$ takes its second branch with $0.02$ probability, $P^*$ and $V$ will carry out a faithful simulation of $M$ on $s$ that will yield a verdict that opposes $P^*$'s with at least $0.5$ probability; therefore $P^*$ will be rejected with at least $0.01$ probability at any segment $s \in \trouble$.

$P^*$ might be able to do better by not being truthful about the safe head's readings.  If it does so on the first round of a segment's simulation, however, $V$ may be in the first branch with $0.99$ probability to reject $P^*$, which is even worse than being truthful throughout the segment.  Instead, $P^*$, by being truthful about the safe head's readings for some rounds of simulation (thus avoiding stark rejection from $V$ that is likely in its first branch) and \emph{somehow} observing whether $V$ is being accurate about the other head's readings (perhaps by having memorized a finite prefix of the input and kept track of the other head's index up to a limit in its finite memory, while still tracking the safe head of $M$ to be able to avoid risking a rejection from the first branch of $V$), can start lying at a later time when it finally becomes the statistically rational choice.  As $P^*$ observes $V$, particularly the symbols $x_{\text{other}}$ it announces, $P^*$ notes either one of the following:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*)]
    \item If $P^*$ \emph{somehow} detects $x_{\text{other}}$ is ``wrong'' (\ie, is not what $M$ would read with its other head from the input), $P^*$ will know for certain that $V$ is on its first branch.  From that point on, a competent $P^*$ will simply continue to provide the readings of the safe head truthfully, which will lead to passing of the segment, while doing anything else leads to certain rejection.
    \item If $P^*$ \emph{somehow} detects $x_{\text{other}}$ is correct, that observation will make $V$ more likely to be in its second branch (from $P^*$'s perspective) by the Bayesian analysis.  Let $Y$ be the random variable indicating the index of $V$'s branch. Then the analysis is as follows:
    \begin{align*}
        \prv{Y = 2 \given x_{\text{other}} \text{ is correct}}
        &= \frac{\prv{x_{\text{other}} \text{ is correct} \given Y = 2} \cdot \prv{Y = 2}}{\prv{x_{\text{other}} \text{ is correct}}}\\
        &= \frac{\prv{x_{\text{other}} \text{ is correct} \given Y = 2} \cdot \prv{Y = 2}}{\sum_{y \in \Set{1, 2}} \prv{x_{\text{other}} \text{ is correct} \given Y = y} \cdot \prv{Y = y}}
    \end{align*}
    However, it is imperative to note that this likelihood can never reach $1$, since the nominator is always strictly smaller than the denominator.
    \item If $P^*$ can neither determine $x_{\text{other}}$ as wrong nor correct, its estimations about $V$'s branch will remain the same.
\end{enumerate}

Thus, whenever $P^*$ decides to tell its lie, it will be taking some non-zero risk of being caught and rejected while doing it.  Let $w_1$ be a segmented input string with which the probability of $P^*$ being rejected is $p > 0$.  Then, with the input string $\paren*{w_1\segsep}^k$, the rejection probability of $P^*$ will become $1 - \paren*{1-p}^k$, which will get arbitrarily closer to $1$ with increasing $k$.

