This fixes our definition of $V$.
Essentially, $V$ challenges its prover to determine each core's membership in $L$ within as many steps as the length of the segment, which, for cores that are adequately padded, is only enough for some \tqfa{}.  $V$ then checks if the prover's claim is true by simulating $M$, which it cannot do by itself and, curiously enough, does so with the help of the prover.  Naturally, $V$ is very careful about trusting this help from the prover, particularly because $V$ may be made to loop indefinitely whenever it trusts the prover.  To that end, $V$ trusts this help with a very low probability $p$, validating that this help is indeed what it is supposed to be with the remaining high probability, $1-p$.

The following analysis will first demonstrate a \tqfa{} $P$ that passes $V$'s tests for an infinite set of input strings with high probability.  Then, we will show that any \tpfa{} $P^*$ will fail same tests for another infinite set of input strings, again with high probability.  Showing that those sets always have an infinite intersection will conclude the proof.

\paragraph{A \tqfa{} prover $P$.}  A \tqfa{} prover $P$ can pass $V$'s tests by doing the following in each segment $s_i$:
\begin{turingenum}
    \item Execute $T$'s algorithm upon the core $x_i$.
    \item If the time was enough to come to a decision, report that decision to $V$.  Otherwise, report either ``member'' or ``not a member'' at random.
    \item Simulate $N_1$ to provide its readings.
\end{turingenum}

The error of failing to accept $P$ can happen either in the form of $V$ running indefinitely or $V$ rejecting $P$.  The only point in $V$'s algorithm that may run indefinitely is \Cref{stg:SIMM}, where it is simulating $M$, where $V$ is following $H_2$ and might be fooled about the $H_1$'s readings.  Since, however, $P$'s algorithm is always honest about $H_1$'s readings, and $M$ is a linear\-/time machine, $V$ will never run indefinitely when paired against $P$.

As for the second way $V$ may fail to accept $P$, we note that \Cref{stg:SIMM} is the only place where $V$ may reject a prover like $P$, \ie, one that honestly tells $N_1$'s readings at \Cref{stg:SIMN1,stg:SIMM}.  $P$ must have also made the wrong claim back at \Cref{stg:ssproversverdict} to be rejected.

Consider the case where the core is ``well\-/padded'' (\ie, $\abs{s_i} \ge 2^{k_T\abs{x_i} + c_T}$).  This guarantees that $P$ will have enough time to complete $T$'s execution, with probability at least $1 - \tau_{k_T,c_T}$.  The probability of $P$ making the wrong decision about such a well\-/padded $x_i$'s membership will therefore be bounded by $\paren*{1 - \tau_{k_T,c_T}}\verr_T + \sfrac{\tau_{k_T,c_T}}{2}$.  If the verifier also takes the branch of \Cref{stg:SIMM} with probability $p$, then $V$ will reject $P$.  The overall probability of this event is %!!!!! BU SATIRIN DEVAMINI SİLSEK NE KAYBEDERİZ????? \utkanadd{UTKAN: Bir noktada $\tau_{k_T,c_T}$ kullanmadan 'almost' diye diye halledebilir miyim demiştim. İyi olmadı, 'almost'ları silip $\tau_{k_T,c_T}$'leri ekledim. Bunu da silmiştim bence ama bazen Overleaf bağlantım kopuyor, herhalde öyle bir ana denk geldi. Şimdi 'almost and'i sildim. Bence 'at most' kalsın, hem sakıncası yok hem de $\verr_T$ ve $\tau_{k_T,c_T}$ upper bound olduklarından eşitlikten bahsetmek doğru olmayabilir.} 
at most
\begin{equation*}
    \paren*{\paren*{1 - \tau_{k_T,c_T}}\verr_T + \sfrac{\tau_{k_T,c_T}}{2}} p \le \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p.
\end{equation*}

Given a string with $m$ segments, each with well\-/padded cores, the probability that $V$ (erroneously) rejects $P$ is at most
\begin{equation*}
    1 - \paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}^m.
\end{equation*}
An upper bound for $m$ that would constrain this error to be no more than $\verr_V$ can be calculated as follows:
\begin{align*}
    1 - \paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}^m &\le \verr_V\\
    1 - \verr_V &\le \paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}^m\\
    \log_{\paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}}\paren*{1 - \verr_V} &\ge m
\end{align*}

Finally (for $P$), for any error bound $\verr_V$, the following describes a set of infinitely many strings that, when given to $V$ as input, will lead to $P$ being accepted with a probability at least $1 - \verr_V$:
\begin{equation*}
    S^+_T = \Set{\lend s_1\segsep \dotsm \segsep s_m \rend | \begin{multlined}
    \forall x_i \in \Sigma^*,
    \forall y_i \in \Set{\padsymb}^*,
    \forall m \in \mathbb{N}, \\
    s_i = x_iy_i,
    \abs{s_i} \ge 2^{k_T\abs{x_i} + c_T},
    m \le \log_{\paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}}\paren*{1 - \verr_V}
    \end{multlined}
    }
\end{equation*}


