\paragraph{Any \tpfa{} prover $P^*$.}  The error of failing to reject a \tpfa{} prover $P^*$ can happen either in the form of $V$ running indefinitely or $V$ accepting.  The only stage $V$ may run indefinitely is \Cref{stg:SIMM}, like with $P$, but this time a $P^*$ may indeed cause $V$ run indefinitely.  The first condition for this occurrence is that $P^*$ must be providing false symbols as $H_1$'s readings during a segment, and the second one is that $V$ must have chosen the branch of \Cref{stg:SIMM} to verify $P^*$ on that segment.  The probability of this event is at most $p$.  It is important to note that $V$ would reject $P^*$ immediately, had it chosen the branch of \Cref{stg:SIMN1}, so there is no ``second chance'' for $P^*$ once it is wrong about $H_1$'s readings.  Therefore, $p$ is the upper bound of $V$ running forever when paired against any $P^*$.  We fix a $p$ that is less than $\verr_V$ to stay within $V$'s error budget.

Regarding the second way $V$ may fail to reject a $P^*$, first, let us examine what $L$ being a member of $\setneg{\TPFA<{2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim>}$ means:
\begin{gather*}
    \phantom{\iff} L \in \setneg{\TPFA<{2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim>}\\
    \iff L \notin \TPFA<{2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim>\\\displaybreak[0]
    \iff \neg \paren*{L \in \TPFA<{2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim>}\\
    \iff \neg \paren*{\exists R\colon \text{${2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim$ \tpfa{}}; \forall z\colon \text{string}; \prv{\text{$R$ miscalculates $z \in L$}} \le \sfrac13.}\\
    \iff \forall R\colon \text{${2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim$ \tpfa{}}; \exists z\colon \text{string}; \prv{\text{$R$ miscalculates $z \in L$}} > \sfrac13.
\end{gather*}
In the last sentence, if such $z$ were finitely many for a \tpfa{} $R$, then a \tpfa{} $\hat{R}$ could first deterministically check if its input is among such $z$, decide their memberships directly through a lookup table if so, and execute like $R$ otherwise.  However, $\hat{R}$ would then be a ${2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim$ \tpfa{} contradicting the last sentence.  Therefore, there must be infinitely many such $z$:
\begin{equation}
    \implies \forall R\colon \text{${2^{\OH{n}}}\rex\tim$ \tpfa{}}; \exists Z\colon \text{infinite set of strings}; \forall z \in Z; \prv{\text{$R$ miscalculates $z \in L$}} > \sfrac13. \label[sentence]{eq:2pfaproverconstraint}
\end{equation}
% \intertext{Finally, let us expand upon the first quantification to reach the following:}
    % \begin{multlined}
    %     \iff \forall k, c, n_0; \forall R_{k,c,n_0}\colon \text{\tpfa{} halting in at most $2^{kn + c}$ steps on inputs with $n_0$ or more symbols};\\
    %     \exists Z\colon \text{infinite set of strings}; \forall z \in Z; \prv{\text{$R_{k,c,n_0}$ miscalculates $z \in L$}} > \sfrac13.
    % \end{multlined}

Now, let $P^*$ be any \tpfa{} prover (of any time bound), and then consider the case where the core $x_i$ has at least $n_0$ symbols and is not overly padded (satisfying $\abs{s_i} \le 2^{k\abs{x_i} + c}$ for some constants $k$ and $c$).  We will then have the following two cases to consider:
\begin{enumerate}[label=Case \arabic*:, font=\bfseries, itemindent=\widthof{\textbf{Case 1.}}+\labelsep-\parindent, leftmargin=\parindent]
    \item $P^*$ runs in expected ${2^{\OH{n}}}$ time.
    This implies, through \Cref{eq:2pfaproverconstraint}, that there are infinitely many $x_i$'s on which $P^*$ has more than $\sfrac13$ probability of being mistaken about their membership in $L$.
    \item $P^*$ does not run in expected ${2^{\OH{n}}}$ time.
    This immediately implies that $P^*$ does not run in strict $2^{\OH{n}}$ time, which in turn implies the existence of infinitely many\footnote{If there were finitely many such strings, they would have a maximum length $n_{\max}$, and $P^*$ would be running within strict ${2^{\OH{n}}}$ time (for a cutoff length larger than $n_{\max}$)---a contradiction.} $x_i$'s such that $P^*$, executing over those $x_i$'s, cannot come to any conclusion regarding their memberships within $2^{k\abs{x_i} + c}$ steps, no matter how large the constants $k$ and $c$ are chosen.  On such $x_i$'s, $P^*$ will have to make a random guess when $V$ asks for its claim in $\abs{s_i}$ ($\le 2^{k\abs{x_i} + c}$) steps and therefore will be mistaken about their membership with $\sfrac12$ probability.  
\end{enumerate}

In either case, there is an infinite set of cores whose memberships $P^*$ will be mistaken about with a probability at least $\sfrac13$, so long as they have such limited padding.  $V$ will check on $P^*$'s claim with probability $p$, so the overall probability of $P^*$'s rejection with such cores with limited padding is at least
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{p}{3}.
\end{equation*}

Given a string with $m$ segments, each with a core that has at least $n_0$ symbols and is not overly padded, the probability that $V$ (erroneously) accepts $P^*$ is at most
\begin{equation*}
    \paren*{1 - \sfrac{p}{3}}^m.
\end{equation*}
A lower bound for $m$ that would constrain this error to be no more than $\verr_V$, can be calculated as follows:
\begin{align*}
    \paren*{1 - \sfrac{p}{3}}^m &\le \verr_V\\
    m &\ge \log_{\paren*{1 - \sfrac{p}{3}}} \verr_V
\end{align*}

Finally (for $P^*$), for any error bound $\verr_V$, the following describes a set of infinitely many strings that, when given to $V$ as input, will lead to $P^*$ being rejected with a probability at least $1 - \verr_V$:
\begin{equation*}
    S^-_{k, c} = \Set{\lend s_1\segsep \dotsm \segsep s_m \rend | \begin{multlined}
    \forall x_i \in \Sigma^*,
    \forall y_i \in \Set{\padsymb}^*,
    \forall m \in \mathbb{N}, \\
    s_i = x_iy_i,
    \abs{x_i} \ge n_0,
    \abs{s_i} \le 2^{k\abs{x_i} + c},
    m \ge \log_{\paren*{1 - \sfrac{p}{3}}} \verr_V
    \end{multlined}
    }
\end{equation*}

The intersection of $S^+_T$ and $S^-_{k, c}$ is as follows:
\begin{equation*}
    S_{T,k,c} = S^+_T \cap S^-_{k, c} = \Set{\lend s_1\segsep \dotsm \segsep s_m \rend | \begin{multlined}
    \forall x_i \in \Sigma^*,
    \forall y_i \in \Set{\padsymb}^*,
    \forall m \in \mathbb{N}, \\
    s_i = x_iy_i,
    \abs{x_i} \ge n_0,
    2^{k_T\abs{x_i} + c_T} \le \abs{s_i} \le 2^{k\abs{x_i} + c},\\
    \log_{\paren*{1 - \sfrac{p}{3}}} \verr_V \le m \le \log_{\paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}}\paren*{1 - \verr_V}
    \end{multlined}
    }
\end{equation*}

To make $S_{T,k,c}$ an infinite set, we will choose the parameters $k_T$, $c_T$, $k$, $c$, $\verr_T$, and $\tau_{k_T,c_T}$ so that the constraints
\begin{gather}
    2^{k_T\abs{x_i} + c_T} \le \abs{s_i} \le 2^{k\abs{x_i} + c}\label[constraint]{cstr:properpadding}
\shortintertext{and}
    \log_{\paren*{1 - \sfrac{p}{3}}} \verr_V \le m \le \log_{\paren*{1 - \paren*{\verr_T + \tau_{k_T,c_T}} p}}\paren*{1 - \verr_V}\label[constraint]{cstr:propernumsegments}
\end{gather}
can be satisfied for some $\abs{s_i}$ and $m$ given any $x_i \in \Sigma^*$.  Then, simply the infinitely different strings $x_i$ can be (that are at least $n_0$ symbols long) will make $S_{T,k,c}$ an infinite set.

