For a matrix $A$, let $\MASD(A)$ be the maximum absolute determinant among all square submatrices of $A$. A standard fact~\cite{cgst1986sensitivity} in the theory of integer programming is that if $P \subseteq \Rbb^n$ is a polyhedron, $P \cap \Zbb^n$ is non-empty, and $x$ is in $P$, then there exists a point $y$ in $P \cap \Zbb^n$ such that $\|x - y\| \le n \MASD(A)$.
